// Static type checking for the Python layers. Gating in lint.yml.
//
// mode: `standard`, not basedpyright's stricter default `recommended`.
//   Measured: standard is 0 errors on the source, recommended is 126 — 104 of
//   them reportTypedDictNotRequiredAccess, i.e. every result["id"] flagged
//   because FixtureResult is total=False. Ratcheting to recommended is a
//   deliberate follow-up, not the price of turning any checking on.
//
// scope: source only. tests/ is intentionally out: it fakes provider clients
//   and MCP responses with duck-typed stand-ins and feeds deliberate edge
//   values (None, empty payloads), which a checker reads as ~120 errors in
//   `standard` mode that are not bugs — a test that calls a renamed function
//   fails under pytest on the next line anyway. The value here is catching a
//   renamed key or bad call in the code that ships — e.g. FixtureResult across
//   its five readers.
//
// exclude vs ignore, and why BOTH name tests/: `exclude` keeps the CI
//   whole-project run from analysing a path, but an editor's language server
//   still type-checks any file you OPEN even when it is excluded — which is why
//   opening a test file used to show errors CI never runs. `ignore` suppresses
//   diagnostics for a path even when open, so the two agree: tests are not
//   type-checked, in CI or in the editor.
//
// pythonVersion: matches .python-version so stdlib and syntax are checked
//   against the interpreter CI actually runs.
{
  "typeCheckingMode": "standard",
  "pythonVersion": "3.14",
  "include": [
    "eval",
    "functional",
    "lane.py",
    "mcp_client.py",
    "ownership.py",
    "toolclass.py",
    "ucp.py",
    "toollint.py"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "**/__pycache__",
    "**/node_modules",
    ".venv",
    "tests"
  ],
  "ignore": [
    "tests"
  ]
}
